            <ArticleTitle>Transient migratory osteoporosis: rapid response to pamidronate treatment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>138-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Transient migratory osteoporosis is characterized by the acute onset of migratory pain at weight-bearing joints. The diagnosis is based on clinical findings together with characteristic abnormalities on MRI or bone scan. Each attack is generally self-limiting but may take several months to resolve. We describe 2 cases of transient migratory osteoporosis to highlight their rapid response to pamidronate therapy; in addition, 1 of these cases affected the sacrum, a site not reported to be involved in this condition.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
